/// The wire contract between the wasmnet host (the patch panel) and every device on the network.
///
/// Model: the host owns every wire. A device never imports another device. It exports `device`
/// (frames *in*) and imports `wire` (frames *out*). All time is virtual: the host passes `now`
/// explicitly, and a device's view of time never moves inside a single call.
///
/// Re-entrancy: the host never calls into a device while that device is inside a call to the host,
/// and no import in this package ever calls back into any device. A frame transmitted at `now`
/// arrives at the peer no earlier than `now + link latency`, as a separate `receive` call.
///
/// Ordering: the host runs one discrete-event queue ordered by virtual time, then a causal key
/// (docs/design/event-order.md). Calls into a device happen in that order, so the same network and the same seed always produce the same calls.
///
/// Push in, push out, and no `stream<frame>`: see docs/design/async-under-virtual-time.md.
package wasmnet:link@0.1.0;

interface types {
    /// A device-local port number. Ports are numbered by the device (see `device.ports`).
    type port-id = u32;

    /// Virtual time in nanoseconds since the start of the simulation. It never goes backwards.
    type instant = u64;

    /// One complete Ethernet frame without preamble or FCS:
    /// `dst(6) src(6) [802.1Q tag(4)] ethertype(2) payload`.
    /// There is no minimum-size padding requirement (a 42-byte ARP frame is fine).
    type frame = list<u8>;

    /// Static description of one port.
    record port-info {
        /// The port number used in `receive`, `link-state` and `wire.transmit`. Unique per device.
        id: port-id,
        /// Human-readable name for diagnostics and pcap interface names (`eth0`, `gi0/1`).
        name: string,
        /// The port's own MAC address (exactly 6 bytes) if it has one. A switch port has none.
        mac: option<list<u8>>,
        /// Largest payload after the Ethernet header (the IP MTU, normally 1500). Unless the
        /// network overrides it, a link's MTU is the smaller of its two ports' MTUs.
        mtu: u32,
    }
}

/// Imported by every device: the outgoing half of the patch panel.
interface wire {
    use types.{port-id, frame};

    /// Queues `frame` for transmission on `port` and returns immediately.
    ///
    /// - Timing: the frame enters the wire at the `now` of the call that is currently running
    ///   (the `now` passed to `receive`/`poll`/`link-state`, or virtual clock time for async
    ///   devices). The peer receives it at `now + latency` as its own, later `receive` call.
    /// - Ownership: the host takes the bytes. The device keeps nothing.
    /// - Re-entrancy: never calls into any device, including this one. It may be called any number
    ///   of times from inside any export call or async task.
    /// - Errors: there are none. The host silently drops (and counts) a frame on an unwired or
    ///   down port, and a frame whose payload exceeds the link MTU, like a NIC with no carrier.
    ///   A frame still in flight when its link goes down is lost. An L2 link drops instead of
    ///   applying backpressure.
    transmit: func(port: port-id, frame: frame);
}

/// Imported by every device: structured diagnostics. The host stamps each record with the
/// current virtual time and the device's name and keeps it in the network's journal.
///
/// Both functions are synchronous and never yield, so they are safe to call from any export call
/// or async task, including while a netstack driver holds its own state (unlike
/// `eprintln!`, whose stdout/stderr write is a yield point). They never call back into a device.
/// A device should keep the volume reasonable: every record is kept for the whole run.
interface log {
    /// Severity of a log line.
    enum level {
        trace,
        debug,
        info,
        warn,
        error,
    }

    /// Records one human-readable log line (no trailing newline needed).
    log: func(level: level, message: string);

    /// Records one structured event. `kind` is a kebab-case name (`mac-learned`, `mac-aged`,
    /// `arp-resolved`). `fields` are key/value pairs. Keys are kebab-case, unique within the
    /// event, and values are plain strings such as `02:00:00:00:00:01` or `3`.
    event: func(kind: string, fields: list<tuple<string, string>>);
}

/// Exported by every device: the incoming half of the patch panel, plus timers for stepped devices.
interface device {
    use types.{port-id, frame, instant, port-info};

    /// Describes the device's ports.
    ///
    /// - Timing: the host calls this exactly once, right after instantiation and before any
    ///   other export (and before any link is wired, so the host can validate port numbers).
    ///   The device's configuration (`wasi:config/store`) is readable at this point. The answer
    ///   must not change afterwards.
    /// - Ownership: the host owns the returned list.
    ports: func() -> list<port-info>;

    /// Delivers one frame that arrived on `port` at virtual time `now`.
    ///
    /// - Timing: `now` is the arrival time (transmit time plus link latency). The device may call
    ///   `wire.transmit` from inside this call, and those frames leave at `now`.
    /// - Ownership: the device owns `frame`.
    /// - Re-entrancy: never called while another *link* export of this device is running, and
    ///   never from inside one of the device's own imports. For an async device this must only
    ///   enqueue and wake a task. It must not block. In a composed node (netstack + apps)
    ///   other calls are in flight at the same time: the apps' `run` and their socket calls.
    /// - After one or more `receive`/`link-state` calls at an instant, the host calls `poll(now)` at
    ///   that same instant (after every other event already queued for it), so the device can
    ///   report a new deadline.
    /// - Malformed frames must be dropped (and counted), never trapped on.
    receive: func(now: instant, port: port-id, frame: frame);

    /// Advances the device's timers to `now` and returns the next instant at which it wants to be
    /// polled, or `none` if it has nothing scheduled.
    ///
    /// - Timing: the host calls `poll(now)` once when the device starts, at every deadline the
    ///   device returned, and after `receive`/`link-state` (see above). A new answer replaces the
    ///   previous deadline. A deadline at or before `now` means "again at this instant", and the
    ///   host bounds how many times that may happen per instant.
    /// - The device may call `wire.transmit` from inside this call.
    /// - Async devices (world `async-device`) that keep time through `wasi:clocks` return `none`.
    poll: func(now: instant) -> option<instant>;

    /// Reports a carrier change on `port` at virtual time `now`.
    ///
    /// - Every port starts down. The host calls `link-state(now, port, true)` when a link is
    ///   attached to the port (for links created before the network runs, at the device's first
    ///   instant, after `ports` and before the first `poll`). A link can also go down and up
    ///   again at scripted instants; both ends then get `link-state` at that instant.
    /// - Only actual changes are reported: never `up` twice in a row for the same port.
    /// - Frames transmitted on a down port are dropped by the host.
    link-state: func(now: instant, port: port-id, up: bool);
}

/// Exported by async devices (nodes): starts their long-lived tasks.
interface lifecycle {
    /// Spawns the device's long-lived tasks (for example a network stack loop and its timers on
    /// virtual `wasi:clocks`) and returns once they are ready.
    ///
    /// - Timing: called once, after `ports` and before any `receive`. Frames that arrive before
    ///   it returns are queued by the host in order.
    /// - The spawned tasks keep running after `start` returns, and may call `wire.transmit` at
    ///   any time. A task woken from `receive` needs wit-bindgen's `inter-task-wakeup` feature.
    /// - It must return. A `start` that never returns is reported as a deadlock.
    start: async func();
}

/// Optionally exported by any device: a read-only snapshot of its counters, for tooling.
///
/// This is a *control call*: the host makes it into a live device, between runs (for example
/// after the network went quiescent), at its current virtual instant. It is not part of the
/// frame path, so it never changes what a network does.
interface counters {
    /// Returns the device's counters as `(name, value)` pairs in a stable order. Names are
    /// kebab-case (`rx-frames`, `timers-armed`).
    ///
    /// - Timing: any time after `ports`. Sync, and it must not block, transmit, log or wake a
    ///   task: reading counters must not perturb the device.
    /// - Ownership: the host owns the returned list.
    counters: func() -> list<tuple<string, u64>>;
}

/// A stepped device: switch, router, NAT, hub, tap or impairment box. It is synchronous, gets time
/// only through `now` arguments and reports its next deadline from `poll`.
///
/// Configuration: the per-device key/value config is served read-only through
/// `wasi:config/store` (`get(key)`, `get-all()` in sorted key order). It never changes during a run.
world stepped-device {
    import wire;
    import log;
    import wasi:config/store@0.2.0-rc.1;
    export device;
}

/// An async device (a node: netstack plus apps). It is the same `device` contract plus `start`.
/// It keeps time through virtual `wasi:clocks` (served by the host) rather than through `poll`.
world async-device {
    include stepped-device;
    export lifecycle;
}

/// An async device that also exports `counters` (the netstack). The host also binds this
/// world, as the superset of all of them: `lifecycle` and `counters` are looked up per instance
/// and are optional.
world counted-async-device {
    include async-device;
    export counters;
}
